 <section xml:id="Section-2">
      <title>Rank-Nullity Theorem</title>

      <p>We define <m>\text{rank}(T)</m> to be the dimension of <m>\text{Im}(T)</m>. We define <m>\text{nullity}(T)</m> to be the dimension of the <m>\text{ker}(T)</m>, also called the <alert>null space</alert> of <m>T</m>.</p>

      <theorem xml:id="theorem-rank-nullity">
        <statement>
          <p>For any linear transformation <m>T: U \to V</m>, where <m>U</m> is finite dimensional, <m>\text{rank}(T) + \text{nullity}(T) = \text{dim}(U)</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>Note that, by the previous lemma, both <m>\text{ker}(T)</m> and <m>\text{Im}(T)</m> are finite dimensional. Take a basis <m>\vec{u}_{1}, \dots, \vec{u}_{m}</m> for <m>\text{ker}(T)</m> and a basis <m>\vec{v}_{1}, \dots, \vec{v}_{k}</m> for <m>\text{Im}(T)</m>. Then, by definition of the image, there exists <m>\vec{w}_{1}, \dots, \vec{w}_{k} \in U</m> such that <m>T(\vec{w}_{i}) = \vec{v}_{i}</m> for all <m>1 \leq i \leq k</m>.</p>

          <p>I claim that <m>\vec{u}_{1}, \dots, \vec{u}_{m}, \vec{w}_{1},\dots, \vec{w}_{k}</m> is a basis for <m>U</m>.</p>

          <p>Note that, by construction, <m>m</m> is dimension of the kernel which is equal to the nullity by definition. Similarly, <m>k</m> is the dimension of the image, which is equal to the rank by definition. If this is a basis, then <m>U</m> has a basis of size <m>k + m</m>, which is exactly the rank plus nullity meaning that <m>\text{dim}(U) = \text{rank}(T) + \text{nullity}(T)</m>.</p>

          <p>Thus, in what remains, all we need to do is prove this is a basis.</p>

          <p>Let's do spanning first. Let <m>\vec{x} \in U</m>. We want to show that</p>
          <me>\vec{x} \in \text{span}(\vec{u}_{1}, \dots, \vec{u}_{m}, \vec{w}_{1}, \dots, \vec{w}_{k}).</me>
          <p>Then <m>T(\vec{x}) \in \text{Im}(T)</m>. Since <m>\vec{v}_{1}, \dots, \vec{v}_{k}</m> is a basis for <m>\text{Im}(T)</m>,</p>
          <me>T(\vec{x}) = \sum_{i=1}^{k}a_{i} \vec{v}_{i}</me>
          <p>for some <m>a_{1}, \dots, a_{k} \in \mathbb{R}</m>.</p>

          <p>Note that</p>
          <me>T\left(\sum_{i=1}^{k} a_{i} \vec{w}_{i} \right) = \sum_{i=1}^{k} a_{i}T(\vec{w}_{i}) = \sum_{i=1}^{k} a_{i} \vec{v}_{i} = T(\vec{x}).</me>

          <p>It follows that</p>
          <me>T(\vec{x}) - T\left(\sum_{i=1}^{k} a_{i} \vec{w}_{i}\right) = T(\vec{x} - \sum_{i=1}^{k} a_{i}\vec{w}_{i}) = \vec{0}.</me>

          <p>Thus, by definition,</p>
          <me>\vec{x} - \sum_{i=1}^{k} a_{i}\vec{w}_{i} \in \text{ker}(T).</me>

          <p>Then, since <m>\vec{u}_{1}, \dots, \vec{u}_{m}</m> are a basis for the kernel,</p>
          <me>\vec{x} - \sum_{i=1}^{k} a_{i}\vec{w}_{i} \in \text{span}(\vec{u}_{1}, \dots, \vec{u}_{m}).</me>

          <p>It follows that there exist <m>b_{1}, \dots, b_{m} \in \mathbb{R}</m> such that</p>
          <me>\vec{x} - \sum_{i=1}^{k} a_{i}\vec{w}_{i} = \sum_{j=1}^{m} b_{j} \vec{u}_{j}.</me>
          <p>By isolating <m>\vec{x}</m>, we find</p>
          <me>\vec{x} = \sum_{i=1}^{k} a_{i}\vec{w}_{i} +\sum_{j=1}^{m} b_{j} \vec{u}_{j}\in \text{span}(\vec{u}_{1}, \dots, \vec{u}_{m}, \vec{w}_{1}, \dots, \vec{w}_{k}).</me>
          <p>Hence, it is spanning.</p>

          <p>Next we need to show independence. Suppose that</p>
          <me>\sum_{i=1}^{k} a_{i}\vec{w}_{i} + \sum_{j=1}^{m} b_{j} \vec{u}_{j} = \vec{0}.</me>
          <p>We want to show that the <m>a_{i}</m> and <m>b_{j}</m> are all then equal to <m>0</m>. The key idea is to rewrite it like this:</p>
          <me>\sum_{i=1}^{k} a_{i}\vec{w}_{i} = -\sum_{j=1}^{m} b_{j} \vec{u}_{j}.</me>

          <p>Since <m>\vec{u}_{1}, \dots, \vec{u}_{m} \in \ker(T)</m> and <m>\ker(T)</m> is a subspace,</p>
          <me>\sum_{i=1}^{k} a_{i}\vec{w}_{i} = -\sum_{j=1}^{m} b_{j} \vec{u}_{j} \in \ker(T).</me>

          <p>Thus,</p>
          <me>\sum_{i=1}^{k} a_{i} \vec{v}_{i} = \sum_{i=1}^{k} a_{i}T(\vec{w}_{i}) = T\left(\sum_{i=1}^{k} a_{i} \vec{w}_{i} \right) = \vec{0}.</me>
          <p>Then, since <m>\vec{v}_{1},\dots, \vec{v}_{k}</m> are a basis, they are in particular independent meaning that all of the <m>a_{i}</m> must be equal to <m>0</m>.</p>

          <p>Thus,</p>
          <me>-\sum_{j=1}^{m} b_{j} \vec{u}_{j} = \vec{0}.</me>
          <p>Since <m>\vec{u}_{1},\dots, \vec{u}_{m}</m> are a basis for <m>\text{ker}(T)</m> they are independent meaning that all <m>b_{j}</m> must also equal <m>0</m>. Hence, by definition <m>\vec{u}_{1}, \dots, \vec{u}_{m}, \vec{w}_{1}, \dots, \vec{w}_{k}</m> are linearly independent. They are also spanning and thus a basis, which completes the proof.</p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>
    </section>